# Code review (`y-agt-code-review`)

Use when the user invokes **`/y-agt-code-review`**, **`@y-agt-code-review`**, or asks for a **code review**, **PR review before push**, or **review of local commits**.

Act as a **senior software engineer**. Assess the work **only** from Git history.

Prose: **B2 English** per [`y-agt-communication-b2-english.mdc`](y-agt-communication-b2-english.mdc).

Design bar and scores: **read and apply** [`y-dev-engineering.mdc`](../dev/y-dev-engineering.mdc) (guidance mode — do not run its standalone review). For ticket local changes, a PR, or a custom scope, use **`/y-dev-engineering`** instead.

---

## Hard limits

- **Review only** commits that exist **locally** but are **not yet on remote `origin`** (unpushed commits).
- **Do not** review **uncommitted** work: no staged, unstaged, or untracked files in scope. **Exception:** with the **Explain changes** outcome, the user may choose to include uncommitted work at the uncommitted-changes gate (useful for AI edits that are not committed yet).
- **Read-only:** no edits, fixes, new commits, or push unless the user clearly asks for separate changes in the same session.

---

## Workflow

0. **Outcome** — **`AskQuestion`** first (skip only when the user already said which one):

| Option | You get |
|--------|---------|
| **Review** (default) | Verdict, scores, blocking items, follow-ups, risks — see [Output — chat](#output--chat) and [Output — markdown report](#output--markdown-report) |
| **Explain changes** | What changed and why, one change at a time with code blocks — no verdict or scores. See [Output — changes explained](#output--changes-explained) |
| **Both** | Review **and** the change walkthrough (two files) |

1. `git fetch origin` (when network allows).
2. Run in parallel where useful:

```bash
git branch --show-current
git status --short
git log @{upstream}..HEAD --oneline
```

3. **Find commits to review:**
   - Prefer: `git log @{upstream}..HEAD` when upstream is set and tracks `origin/...`.
   - If upstream is not set: `git log origin/$(git branch --show-current)..HEAD` **only if** `origin/<current-branch>` exists.
   - If still unclear: compare to `origin/testing` or the base branch named in the user message.
   - If you **cannot** determine a fair comparison to **origin**, say so briefly — **do not** review uncommitted diffs instead.

4. Review the **combined diff** (`git diff <base>..HEAD` or per-commit `git show`), not the whole repository. **State the comparison base** in chat and in any report.

5. **Uncommitted changes** — if `git status` shows staged, unstaged, or untracked files, **`AskQuestion`** before reviewing:

| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| **Review unpushed commits only** (default) | Ignore working tree |
| **Include uncommitted changes** | Only for **Explain changes** — add staged, unstaged, and untracked task files to the walkthrough. Not offered for **Review**; with **Both**, the review part still uses unpushed commits only |
| **Stop — I will commit first** | End review; user commits then re-runs |
| **`other`** | User explains different intent |

   If there are **no unpushed commits** but there is uncommitted work and the outcome is **Explain changes**, offer **Include uncommitted changes** as the default instead of stopping.

Use **`AskQuestion`** for every gate — no chat-only confirmation (same pattern as [`y-git-commits.mdc`](../git/y-git-commits.mdc) § AskQuestion gates).

---

## What to check (prioritize by risk)

For the **Review** and **Both** outcomes. **Explain changes** alone skips this section.

Review the diff against the categories below. **Do not** repeat the same finding under multiple headings — pick the best fit (usually **Design & scope** or **Blocking**).

### Behavior

| Check | Look for |
|-------|----------|
| **Correctness** | Happy path, null/empty inputs, error paths, concurrency/async when relevant. Each expected failure has a deliberate outcome — safe, visible fallback or clear failure — per [Fallbacks and missing data](../dev/y-dev-engineering.mdc#fallbacks-and-missing-data); flag swallowed errors and silent defaults on important fields |
| **Tests** | Gaps for changed behavior; broken or missing regression coverage |

### Design & scope

Judge this section against [`y-dev-engineering.mdc`](../dev/y-dev-engineering.mdc): simplest correct change, no blind copying of weak patterns, SOLID / DRY / patterns only when they help, no unrelated cleanup. Apply its **Python layout** section only to `.py` files.

| Check | Look for |
|-------|----------|
| **Task scope** | Changes limited to what the ticket needs — no unrelated refactors, drive-by cleanups, or extra files |
| **Reuse & generality** | Existing helpers, patterns, and config before new code **when they are a good fit**; prefer a **generic** solution when the codebase already solves the same shape of problem — flag one-off logic that will duplicate on the next similar ticket. Flag a copy of a weak or legacy pattern made only for consistency |
| **Overfitting** | Hard-coding this ticket's edge case (special cases, magic strings, forked flow) where a small shared rule or parameter would stay maintainable — call it out when the fix only works for this story |
| **Over-engineering** | Premature abstractions, new layers, or frameworks not required by the diff; one-line wrappers; speculative “future-proof” code — prefer the **simplest** change that is correct |
| **Codebase fit** | Respects public APIs, contracts, schemas, and framework boundaries; matches **CONTRIBUTING.md** when present. Fits nearby code without copying its weak parts |

**Severity:** overfitting or over-engineering that blocks merge → **Blocking** or **Request changes**; nice-to-have simplification → **Follow-up**. Do **not** block on style nits when behavior is correct and consistent with nearby code. Python layout findings are follow-ups unless they hide a real defect.

### Security & operations

| Check | Look for |
|-------|----------|
| **Security** | Secrets, injection, authz, PII in logs, unsafe defaults |
| **Operability** | Logging, metrics, config, migrations, backward compatibility |

### Contracts

| Check | Look for |
|-------|----------|
| **Docs & API** | Public behavior or integrations that need doc/contract updates |

---

## Output — chat

- Be **direct**: **blocking** vs **follow-ups** vs **nits** — one home per issue (see **Design & scope** above).
- Point to **files/lines** when possible.

**Verdict:** Approve | Approve with follow-ups | Request changes

### Scores

Always give **`## Scores`** right after the **Verdict**, in chat and in any report. Use the six metrics, format, anchors, and verdict rules from [`y-dev-engineering.mdc` § Review scores](../dev/y-dev-engineering.mdc#review-scores) — do not copy the rubric here.

### When everything looks good

Reply in chat with verdict, one-line summary, **Scores**, and any **residual risks** only. **No file required.**

---

## Output — markdown report

When there is **blocking** feedback, **follow-ups**, or **residual risks**, write a **concise markdown file** per **`y-agt-workspace`** and the policy below.

### Report artifact policy

- Save under **`cursor_workspace/code-review/`** only — **not** under `docs/` or other repo paths.
- **`mkdir -p`** before write; **do not overwrite** previous reports.
- **Filename:** all **lowercase** — `code-review-<jira-key>-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>-<branch-slug>.md`
  - **jira-key:** lowercase issue key (e.g. `vap-3547`); use **`no-ticket`** when none inferred from branch/commits.
  - **YYYYMMDD-HHMM:** 24-hour **local** time when the report is written (minute precision; e.g. `20260805-1256`).
  - **branch-slug:** lowercase kebab-case from the current branch.
- **Example filename:** `code-review-vap-3547-20260805-1256-feature-vap-3547-explain-insight.md`

### Document title (H1)

**Pattern:** `Code Review - <JIRA> - <YYYYMMDD-HHMM> - <branch-slug>`

```markdown
# Code Review - VAP-3547 - 20260805-1256 - feature-vap-3547-explain-insight
```

- H1 may use normal Jira casing (`VAP-3547`); **filename stays lowercase**.
- **`<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>`** and **branch slug** in the H1 must match the filename.

Keep the doc **short** (roughly one screen):

```markdown
# Code Review - VAP-3547 - 20260805-1256 - feature-vap-3547-explain-insight

**Branch:** …
**Base:** …
**Commits reviewed:** N (hashes)
**Verdict:** Approve | Approve with follow-ups | Request changes

## Scores
(six metrics per y-dev-engineering Review scores)

## Scope
(table or bullet list of commits + files)

## What works well
(bullets — only if useful)

## Blocking
(numbered — must fix before merge; omit section if none)

## Follow-ups
(numbered — non-blocking; design simplifications, reuse, or generic refactor ideas go here; omit if none)

## Residual risks
(bullets — omit if none)
```

Also give a **brief chat summary** with verdict, scores, and the file path.

---

## Output — changes explained

For the **Explain changes** and **Both** outcomes. **Goal:** a reader who did not write the code (AI changes or a teammate's) understands **what** changed, **why**, and **what it affects** — easily, accurately, and completely. Explain only; no verdict or scores here.

Give the **same walkthrough in chat and in the file**. Always write the file, even for a small diff.

### How to write it

- **Group by logical change**, not by line. One change = one idea (for example "add retry on timeout"), even if it spans several hunks or files.
- **Order by flow:** the main change first, then what supports it (config, tests, docs). Not alphabetical.
- **Show code:** each change gets a ` ```diff ` block with the relevant hunk only (`+` / `-` lines and a little context). Trim long hunks with `…`; never invent or rewrite code.
- **Short, plain descriptions:** one or two sentences each for **What** and **Why**. Common words. Explain project terms the first time.
- **Why must be honest:** take it from commit messages, the ticket, comments, or code context. When you infer it, mark it **(inferred)**. Never make up a reason.
- **Complete:** every changed file appears. Group trivial edits (formatting, renames, import order) into one **Small changes** item that lists the files.
- Point out behavior changes, breaking changes, new config or env vars, migrations, and removed code. Do not judge quality here.
- No secrets in code blocks — replace values with `…`.

### File

- Path: `cursor_workspace/code-review/code-changes-<jira-key>-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>-<branch-slug>.md` — same naming rules as the review report (lowercase, `mkdir -p`, do not overwrite).
- H1: `Code Changes - <JIRA> - <YYYYMMDD-HHMM> - <branch-slug>` (timestamp and slug match the filename).

````markdown
# Code Changes - VAP-3547 - 20260805-1256 - feature-vap-3547-explain-insight

**Branch:** …
**Base:** …
**Included:** N unpushed commits (hashes) | plus uncommitted changes

## Overview
(2–4 sentences: what this change set does as a whole, and why)

## Changes

### 1. <Short title of the change>

**Files:** `path/to/file.py` (L10–L42)
**What:** <one or two plain sentences>
**Why:** <one or two plain sentences; mark (inferred) when not stated>
**Affects:** <behavior, API, config, data — omit when nothing outside the code changes>

```diff
- old line
+ new line
```

### 2. …

### N. Small changes
- `path/a.py` — import order
- `path/b.md` — typo

## Coverage
| File | Change |
|---|---|
| `path/to/file.py` | 1 |
| `path/a.py` | N |
````

**Chat:** the same Overview and Changes, then the file path. With **Both**, give the review summary (verdict and scores) first, then the walkthrough, then both file paths.

---

## Do not

- Review the whole repo or uncommitted diffs as a substitute when unpushed-commit scope cannot be determined (the **Explain changes** opt-in for uncommitted work is the only exception).
- Produce long essays or duplicate the full diff in the report. In the change walkthrough, show focused hunks per change — not the raw full diff.
- Invent a **Why** for a change, or skip a changed file in the walkthrough.
- Auto-fix issues as part of the review task.
